---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import Photo from '../../components/Photo.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import AsideNav from '../../components/AsideNav.astro';
import { IMG } from '../../images';
const path = '/data-sanitization/clear-purge-destroy';
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Data Sanitization', href: '/data-sanitization' },
  { name: 'Clear, Purge, Destroy', href: path },
];
---
<Base
  title="Clear, Purge, Destroy, Verify: NIST 800-88 Sanitization Methods"
  description="NIST 800-88 Rev 1 defines three levels of data sanitization: Clear, Purge and Destroy. How each works, when to use it, and how results are verified."
  path={path}
  crumbs={crumbs}
  image={IMG.destroyFragments.src}
>
  <PageHero crumbs={crumbs} eyebrow="NIST 800-88 Rev 1" title="Clear, Purge, Destroy, Verify" lead="The three levels of data sanitization, and how we verify the result." />
  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="wrap with-aside">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Classification of data</h2>
        <p>
          The first step to methodical data destruction is classifying the type, and therefore the security level, of the data. This
          sets the level of sanitization required and the methods to use.
        </p>
        <p>
          Organizations with limited internal guidelines can take guidance from a
          <a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/sp800" rel="noopener">series of NIST publications</a>, in particular SP 800-60
          Vol 1 Rev 1. See also our <a href="/data-sanitization/data-classification">data classification</a> page.
        </p>
        <p>
          When deciding which security level describes your information, think about its value and confidentiality, and the
          consequences of losing it. Your clients' names and home addresses may not seem "high security" to you, but they may be to
          your clients, employees or other stakeholders. Use this security level to decide how the storage media should be handled.
        </p>

        <div class="split split--top" style="gap: 24px;">
          <div class="stack" style="flex-basis: 420px;">
            <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Clear</h2>
            <p>
              Software or hardware products overwrite user-addressable storage space with non-sensitive data, using the device's
              standard read and write commands. This should cover not only the logical location of files but all user-addressable
              locations, replacing target data with non-sensitive data.
            </p>
          </div>
          <Photo img={IMG.erasure} alt="Binary data being swept away" />
        </div>
        <p>
          Overwriting cannot be used on damaged or non-rewritable media, and may not reach every area where sensitive data is
          retained. Flash-based storage, for example, may contain spare cells and use wear levelling, so not every area can be
          addressed through the normal interface.
        </p>
        <p>
          For devices other than dedicated storage, such as a basic mobile phone or office equipment, a manufacturer reset may be the
          only option. It still counts as Clear as long as the device interface does not allow the cleared data to be retrieved.
        </p>

        <div class="split split--top" style="gap: 24px;">
          <div class="stack" style="flex-basis: 420px;">
            <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Purge</h2>
            <p>
              Purge methods include overwrite, block erase and Cryptographic Erase, using dedicated, standardized device sanitize
              commands that bypass the abstraction of normal read and write commands.
            </p>
          </div>
          <Photo img={IMG.degausserTs1} alt="TS-1 degausser used to purge magnetic media" />
        </div>
        <p>
          Destructive techniques also purge a device when applied to the right media: incineration, shredding, disintegrating,
          degaussing and pulverizing all make recovery infeasible with state-of-the-art laboratory techniques. Bending, cutting and
          some emergency procedures (such as shooting a hole through a drive) may only damage the media, leaving portions readable in
          a laboratory.
        </p>
        <p>
          Degaussing purges legacy magnetic devices when the degausser's strength is matched to the media's coercivity. It should never
          be relied on alone for flash-based storage, or for magnetic storage that also holds non-volatile, non-magnetic storage. Because
          it makes many devices unusable, degaussing is often also a destruction technique.
        </p>

        <div class="split split--top" style="gap: 24px;">
          <div class="stack" style="flex-basis: 420px;">
            <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Destroy</h2>
            <p>
              There are many destruction techniques. Some make target data impossible to retrieve through the device interface, but a
              device only counts as destroyed when retrieval is infeasible even with state-of-the-art laboratory techniques.
            </p>
          </div>
          <Photo img={IMG.destroyFragments} alt="Storage media shredded into small fragments" />
        </div>
        <p>
          Destruction may be the only option when media has failed and Clear or Purge cannot be applied, or when verification of
          Clear or Purge fails for known or unknown reasons.
        </p>

        <div class="split split--top" style="gap: 24px;">
          <div class="stack" style="flex-basis: 420px;">
            <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Verify</h2>
            <p>Verification is needed for techniques whose results are not visible to the naked eye, such as degaussing and software erasure.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>A degaussed hard drive shows no response at all when plugged into its native interface.</li>
              <li>An erased hard drive can be examined with a hex editor to confirm all data has been overwritten. This check is usually built into the erasure software.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <Photo img={IMG.verifyHex} alt="Hex editor view of an overwritten drive" />
        </div>
      </article>
      <AsideNav section="/data-sanitization" path={path} />
    </div>
  </section>
  <CtaBand />
</Base>
